From ff23e9debb5f9413fb69d5465050099d1a16efda Mon Sep 17 00:00:00 2001
From: Arena Agent <agent@arena.ai>
Date: Sat, 10 Oct 2026 00:46:54 +0000
Subject: [PATCH 01/29] Mali-G615: add the GPU safety guard

A Steam session on the Mali-G615 did not fail, it took the phone with it:
Android recorded the next boot as kernel_panic, and the trace named the
kernel's own module (CS_FATAL / CS_BUS_FAULT -> FENCE_SIGNAL timeout(3000 ms)
-> "Unexpected kernel BRK exception at EL1" in kbasep_csf_sync_kcpu_dump_locked,
mali_kbase_mt6897_r44).

An APK cannot repair that module, cannot read the kernel log, and is not
running when it happens. So the guard does the four things an app can do.

1. Break the loop (BootReason, the latch in GpuGuard). sys.boot.reason is
   read at every app start and counted once per boot - elapsedRealtime() is
   stored on each start and a new boot is detected by the uptime going
   backwards, so opening the app ten times after one crash counts one crash.
   A fatal boot reason engages the latch: every launch is refused until the
   user re-arms, and an automatic start is refused with no dialog at all, so
   opening the app can never put the device back in the loop. Three sessions
   ending normally lift the lock; a session the guard stopped resets it.

2. Ask less of the GPU (SafeProfile). Safe caps the output at 720p and the
   frame rate at 30 and turns off HDR, frame generation, mangoapp, the realtime
   queues and the clock pin. Strict adds 576p/24, no shader cache, no upscaler
   or effects, no second screen, and the client interface on llvmpipe - which
   is a diagnosis as much as a restriction.

3. Test before Steam does (Probe, droiddeck-gpu-probe). Enumeration under
   timeout, then a ctypes vkCmdFillBuffer plus a fence wait against the
   session's own ICD, then a readback. Only a fence that never signals is a
   failure; anything that stops the test being built is a skip, so a bug in
   the probe can never lock someone out of their own phone.

4. Stop the session first (GpuWatchdog). Frames, processes wedged in D state
   (3+ of our own uid for 3 samples, ~1.5 s, inside the kernel's own 3 s fence
   timeout), and temperature. An abort sweeps the tree four seconds later.

Everything is recorded: gpu-guard.log and gpu-probe.log per session, a GPU
safety section in device.txt, and a fifth Setup tab (Safety, on Mali) with a
Copy button for bug reports. Strings in English and Spanish.

Full write-up, including what the guard deliberately does not do, in
docs/gpu-safety.md.

